<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
	<dict>
		<!--
			Required by MSAL.

			MSAL keeps its token cache in the iOS keychain, under the access group
			com.microsoft.adalcache unless told otherwise. An app whose entitlements do not grant
			that group cannot write there, so the first token save fails and sign-in reports:

				MsalClientException: missing_entitlements
				The application does not have keychain access groups enabled in the
				Entitlements.plist ... the keychain access group
				'{TeamId}.com.microsoft.adalcache' is not enabled.

			(On some MSAL versions the failure comes even earlier, as
			cannot_access_publisher_keychain, when MSAL probes the keychain to work out the
			publisher's Team ID while building the IPublicClientApplication.)

			$(AppIdentifierPrefix) is substituted at build time with the Team ID from the
			provisioning profile, so the group resolves to {TeamId}.com.microsoft.adalcache - the
			value MSAL derives on its own, which is why no matching
			.WithIosKeychainSecurityGroup(...) call is needed in code. Development provisioning
			profiles carry keychain-access-groups {TeamId}.*, so this signs without any extra
			capability; a distribution build needs Keychain Sharing enabled on the App ID.

			Sharing this group is also what gives single sign-on between apps signed by the same
			team that use it - the group is team-prefixed, so it never reaches another
			publisher's apps. To keep the cache private to this app,
			use $(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier) here and pass the same value to
			MSAL from App.xaml.cs - the Uno.Extensions provider owns the
			IPublicClientApplication, so the way in is the builder callback:

				auth.AddMsal(
				    window,
				    configure: msal => msal.Builder(
				        pca => pca.WithIosKeychainSecurityGroup("{BundleId}")),
				    name: "MsalAuthentication")

			The bundle's own group is listed first on purpose: once this entitlement exists, its
			first entry becomes the default access group for every keychain item the app writes
			without naming one, and listing com.microsoft.adalcache alone would quietly move
			those into the shared group. Nothing else in this head writes to the keychain - on a
			Skia head Uno.Extensions' key-value storage is ApplicationData, not
			KeyChainKeyValueStorage - but a native iOS head does keep ITokenCache there, so this
			ordering is the one to copy.

			See https://aka.ms/msal-net-enable-keychain-groups
		-->
		<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
		<array>
			<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
			<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.microsoft.adalcache</string>
		</array>
	</dict>
</plist>
